Dijkstra's algorithm finds the shortest paths from a source vertex to all other vertices in a weighted graph with non-negative edge weights.
Algorithm
1. Set the source distance to 0.
2. Set all other distances to infinity.
3. Select the unvisited vertex with the smallest distance.
4. Update the distances of its adjacent vertices.
5. Mark the selected vertex as visited.
6. Repeat until all reachable vertices have been processed.
C program
#include <stdio.h>

#define INF 9999

int main() {
    int a[10][10], d[10], vis[10] = {0};
    int n, s, u, min;

    scanf("%d", &n);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &a[i][j]);

            if (i != j && a[i][j] == 0)
                a[i][j] = INF;
        }

    scanf("%d", &s);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        d[i] = INF;

    d[s] = 0;

    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
        min = INF;
        u = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            if (!vis[i] && d[i] < min) {
                min = d[i];
                u = i;
            }

        if (u == -1)
            break;

        vis[u] = 1;

        for (int v = 0; v < n; v++)
            if (!vis[v] && a[u][v] != INF &&
                d[u] + a[u][v] < d[v])
                d[v] = d[u] + a[u][v];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d -> %d = %d\n", s, i, d[i]);

    return 0;
}

Example
Consider the graph:
- A–B = 4
- A–C = 1
- C–B = 2
- B–D = 1
- C–D = 5
Take A as the source.
Vertex	Shortest distance from A
A	0
B	3
C	1
D	4


Shortest paths:
- A → A = 0
- A → C = 1
- A → C → B = 3
- A → C → B → D = 4
Note: These example values illustrate the algorithm; they are not the final distances for the handwritten graphs.
